<section class="atl-about inv-about">
  <h2 class="ref-sec-title">How to read the tree</h2>
  <p>Each card is an invention or discovery, placed at its first known date, the earliest firm evidence historians accept, which is often earlier than the version that spread. A line joins it to what it built on. Time runs left to right, but not evenly: each age gets room by how many inventions it holds, so a few centuries of the industrial age are as wide as tens of thousands of years of prehistory. &ldquo;Credited to&rdquo; names the society or state where it first appeared and the people the sources name; many inventions arose more than once, and where the record shows that, the card says so. Dates and credits come from the encyclopedia entry of each invention and the works it cites, and they are debated more often than textbooks suggest: the card says so where they are.</p>
